<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarikh-i Rashidi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1541">1541</a>–<a href="/years/1546">1546</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tarikh-i Rashidi is a Persian history written between 1541 and 1546 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-muhammad-haidar-dughlat">Mirza Muhammad Haidar Dughlat</a> and named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rashid-khan">Abd al-Rashid Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a>. It records the history of the Chagatai khans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a> and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlat</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a>, together with the author's memoirs of campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It is the main source for Moghulistan and the early Yarkent Khanate.</p></div>
